          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p A functor has a left multiadjoint if and only if it factors (essentially uniquely) as a right adjoint functor followed by a discrete fibration.
          %p
            Since both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            functors and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibrations"} discrete fibrations
            have left multiadjoints, one direction follows from the closure of functors having left multiadjoints under composition.
          %p
            In the other direction, every functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            factors into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+functor"} final functor
            followed by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibration"} discrete fibration
            via the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comprehensive+factorisation"} comprehensive factorisation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            has a left multiadjoint, the final functor will in addition be right adjoint.
          %p
            For a complete proof, see (
            %a{:href => "#Diers1980"}> Diers 80, Proposition 1.1
            ).
